Modern space operations generate huge volumes of heterogeneous data from satellites, drones, ground stations, and mission control systems creating a massive challenge for timely, trusted, and explainable decision-making. Battle Management Command, Control, and Communication (BMC3) systems, including the U.S. Space Force’s National Space Defense Center (NSDC), face a critical need for integrated, verifiable frameworks that can convert this distributed, high-velocity data into actionable knowledge for mission assurance, runtime monitoring, and fault detection.
Currently, the space domain’s decision infrastructure is limited by fragmented data pipelines, disconnected models, and slow validation and verification (V&V) processes. Thermal, communications, and operational data streams remain siloed across multiple systems and vendors, with limited semantic alignment. Testing and certification cycles take months, delaying deployment of new algorithms, models, or tools to operational environments. Agencies such as NSDC and DARPA’s JADC2 programs have noted persistent pain points in interoperability, explainability, and rapid validation of autonomous systems.
This Seedling project proposes to address these gaps through an ontology-driven, contract-based framework for autonomous decision support in space systems. The framework begins with data integration as its foundation, leveraging ontologies and knowledge graphs to unify multi-domain, multi-vendor information into a coherent semantic layer. By aligning data from satellite telemetry, ground networks, and mission simulations within a common ontological structure, the system enables consistent reasoning across heterogeneous sources and ensures traceability from data to decision.
At the core of the framework lies the Contract Engine, which formalizes mission assumptions, constraints, and verification logic as executable contracts. These contracts operate at both design-time and runtime to continuously monitor conformance, detect faults, and validate model behavior against mission requirements. This mechanism bridges engineering and operations by ensuring that every data-driven or AI-driven decision can be traced back to explicit, testable rules.
To enhance scalability and adaptability, the framework incorporates Large Language Model (LLM)-assisted analytics that extract, normalize, and contextualize knowledge from unstructured documents such as requirements, procedures, and V&V reports. LLMs act as intelligent assistants for data ingestion and reasoning that can transform natural-language mission specifications into machine-interpretable contracts. Combined with the ontology, this approach supports explainable autonomy: every recommendation or alert produced by the system can be traced through a transparent reasoning path, allowing human operators and reviewers to understand why a decision was made.
The architecture also embeds runtime monitoring and analytics to detect anomalies and health degradations in real time, leveraging telemetry, environmental, and simulation data. Faults are evaluated against predefined contract thresholds, triggering alerts or corrective actions as needed. This capability directly supports Space Force and NSDC objectives for resilient, self-aware systems that maintain operational integrity under uncertain or adversarial conditions.
To ensure security and compliance, the framework adopts Zero Trust and Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) principles. Each data transaction or reasoning operation is authenticated, authorized, and auditable, ensuring that sensitive or classified data remains protected even as it flows through multi-domain environments. This security and governance layer aligns with DoD cybersecurity policies (NIST 800-171, CMMC 2.0, RMF) and provides a foundation for certifiable, trustworthy autonomy.
Innovation and Impact:
•	Ontology-driven data integration: Creates a unified digital backbone across thermal, communication, and mission domains.
•	Contract-based reasoning: Formalizes and verifies mission assumptions, constraints, and behaviors.
•	LLM-assisted knowledge extraction: Converts documentation and logs into structured, actionable knowledge.
•	Runtime monitoring and fault detection: Enables continuous assurance and early anomaly detection.
•	Autonomous decision support: Provides explainable, traceable recommendations for operators and command systems.
•	V&V acceleration: Reduces testing and certification cycle times by automating contract-based checks.
•	Security and governance: Implements Zero-Trust policies and full auditability for compliance and certification.
The project’s Phase I (5 months / $120 K) will focus on building a prototype data-to-decision pipeline. Key deliverables include:
1.	A domain-aligned ontology and knowledge graph integrating at least two data sources (thermal and communications).
2.	A contract schema for representing mission constraints and verification rules.
3.	An initial LLM-assisted data ingestion capability for extracting knowledge from design and test documentation.
4.	A runtime monitoring module demonstrating automated fault detection and contract validation.
5.	Metrics for evaluating data fusion quality, decision latency reduction, and V&V time savings.
Follow-on CRAD applications will target the U.S. Space Force (NSDC) for rapid testing and certification of multi-vendor solutions, NASA for digital-twin and MBSE verification, and AFRL/DARPA for explainable autonomy and battle-management decision aids. The framework directly supports ongoing U.S. efforts to advance Joint All-Domain Command and Control (JADC2) and accelerate the transition from human-driven analysis to trusted, semi-autonomous decision environments.
Ultimately, this Seedling establishes a modular, extensible foundation for autonomous decision support in space operations, aligning RTX’s enterprise ontology and digital-thread initiatives with government priorities in resilient, intelligent, and verifiable mission systems.

